{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "39",
    "case_summary": "A 65-year-old woman, who does not smoke, presented with a 6-month history of persistent dry cough. Imaging studies identified a solitary nodule in the right upper lung field. The patient also reported mild, persistent lumbar pain over the past two years, for which she had been taking nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs. Physical examination revealed a barely palpable mass in the lumbar area, while reflexes in the lower extremities were normal. Laboratory tests showed no abnormalities, and tumor markers were within normal ranges. MRI and CT scans identified an ill-defined osteolytic lesion in the L5 vertebra, featuring splotch calcification, cortical permeation, and extension into surrounding soft tissues. A chest CT scan showed the lung nodule to have similar characteristics. An attempt to biopsy the lung nodule was made, but the sample was insufficient for further analysis. Due to the risk of collapse of the L5 vertebra, the patient underwent spinal surgery, and the mass was excised.\n\nThe histopathological examination revealed a moderately cellular multilobular neoplasm with chondroid and myxoid matrix and focal necrosis. Under high power view, multicellular lacunae of chondrocytes with moderate nuclear pleomorphism and hypercromasia were observed. Mitotic figures were numerous. On immunohistochemical examination > 25 % of the neoplastic cells exhibited nuclear immunoreactivity for the tumor suppressor protein p53. Furthermore < 1 % of the neoplastic cells were in cell cycle, as estimated with the immunohistochemical marker MIB-1 (ki-67).\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n",
